  "raw_text": "Step 1: Recognize that for any complex number \\(z\\), \\(z \\cdot \\overline{z} = |z|^2\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the given expression as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{|r_1|^2} + \\frac{1}{|r_2|^2} + \\frac{1}{|r_3|^2} + \\frac{1}{|r_4|^2} + \\frac{1}{|r_5|^2}.\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Note that the polynomial \\(x^{10} + (13x - 1)^{10}\\) is symmetric in terms of its roots and their conjugates. This means if \\(r\\) is a root, then \\(\\overline{r}\\) is also a root. The polynomial can be written as:\n\\[\nP(x) = x^{10} + (13x - 1)^{10}.\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Consider the polynomial \\(Q(x) = P(x)P(-x)\\):\n\\[\nQ(x) = (x^{10} + (13x - 1)^{10})(-x^{10} + (13x + 1)^{10}).\n\\]\nThis polynomial has roots at all the roots of \\(P(x)\\) and their negatives. Since \\(P(x)\\) has 10 roots, \\(Q(x)\\) will have 20 roots, but due to symmetry, these roots come in pairs of conjugates.\n\nStep 4: The product of the roots of \\(Q(x)\\) taken two at a time (i.e., the product of the roots of \\(P(x)\\) and their negatives) gives us the sum of the reciprocals of the squares of the magnitudes of the roots of \\(P(x)\\). Specifically, the constant term of \\(Q(x)\\) divided by the leading coefficient will give us this sum.\n\nStep 5: Calculate the constant term of \\(Q(x)\\):\n\\[\nQ(0) = (0^{10} + (13 \\cdot 0 - 1)^{10})(0^{10} + (13 \\cdot 0 + 1)^{10}) = (-1)^{10} \\cdot 1^{10} = 1.\n\\]\nThe leading coefficient of \\(Q(x)\\) is \\(1 \\cdot 1 = 1\\).\n\nStep 6: Therefore, the sum of the reciprocals of the squares of the magnitudes of the roots of \\(P(x)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{|r_1|^2} + \\frac{1}{|r_2|^2} + \\frac{1}{|r_3|^2} + \\frac{1}{|r_4|^2} + \\frac{1}{|r_5|^2} = 1.\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{1}<|im_end|>",
